WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.830
A student once told me, “My password is

2
00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:06.770
safe: it’s my name and my birth year.”

3
00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:09.130
Then I opened his profile.

4
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.320
His name was at the top.

5
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.200
And right below it, a photo

6
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.360
of his birthday cake.

7
00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:19.510
Today: how passwords get guessed, and

8
00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:21.150
how to make one nobody can guess.

9
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.360
A password is the key to your

10
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.700
games, your chats and your email.

11
00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:28.970
Accounts aren’t broken into by

12
00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:30.110
people guessing by hand.

13
00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:33.620
Programs try millions of guesses: popular

14
00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:36.140
ones like one two three four five six,

15
00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.260
names, birthdays and football teams.

16
00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:42.310
What slows them down is length.

17
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.300
A lock with three dials has a thousand

18
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.500
combinations; with six dials, a million.

19
00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.360
Every extra character multiplies the work.

20
00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.410
And swapping a letter for a symbol?

21
00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:58.460
Programs know that trick too.

22
00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.090
Here’s the answer: a passphrase.

23
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.300
Pick three or four random words that have

24
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.480
nothing to do with you, and join them.

25
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.880
It’s long, hard to guess

26
00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.620
and easy to picture.

27
00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:16.870
Mango, bicycle, thunder, spoon.

28
00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:20.610
Picture a bicycle full of mangoes in a

29
00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.190
thunderstorm, with a spoon for a bell.

30
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:24.920
You won’t forget it.

31
00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:27.810
Just don’t use ours: make your own.

32
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:29.660
Quick check!

33
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.670
Three passwords: a name with a

34
00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:36.870
year, the word password with

35
00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.030
symbols, and four random words.

36
00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:43.740
Which is hardest for a program to guess?

37
00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.390
Four random words.

38
00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:48.780
They’re long, and they say

39
00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.000
nothing about you.

40
00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:52.440
The other two are among the very

41
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.280
first guesses a program tries.

42
00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.980
One password for everything is risky too.

43
00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.810
If one game website leaks it, criminals

44
00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.150
try it on your email and your chats.

45
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:06.300
Your email matters most,

46
00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.320
because reset links go there.

47
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:11.780
So every account gets its own password.

48
00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.570
Keep passwords secret, even from

49
00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.850
your best friend: friends fall out,

50
00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:19.170
and phones get borrowed.

51
00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.890
A parent or guardian who helps with

52
00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:23.230
your accounts is different.

53
00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:24.890
Too many to remember?

54
00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.280
Use a password manager, or a

55
00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:29.860
notebook kept safe at home.

56
00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.100
On a shared or café

57
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.000
computer, always sign out.

58
00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:35.960
Your turn.

59
00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.680
Your best friend wants your game

60
00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.560
password, to collect your daily

61
00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:41.880
rewards while you’re away.

62
00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:43.600
Do you share it?

63
00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.090
Keep it to yourself: a day of

64
00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:48.790
rewards isn’t worth your account.

65
00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.600
Remember: long random words, a

66
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:53.880
different password for every

67
00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:55.720
account, and never shared.

68
00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.580
Try it tomorrow: pick four random words

69
00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.160
and picture one funny scene that links

70
00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:02.420
them.

71
00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.450
If you still remember it at bedtime, make

72
00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.550
a new passphrase of your own for your

73
00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:09.630
email, with a parent’s help if you like.
